.TH "vsearch-fastq_mergepairs" "1" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --fastq_mergepairs \[em] merge paired-end reads into one
sequence
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] \f[I]fwdfile\f[R]
\f[B]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]revfile\f[R] (\f[B]--fastaout\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastqout\f[R] | \f[I]other output options\f[R])
\f[I]filename\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] merges paired-end
sequence reads into a single sequence by aligning the forward and
reverse reads and combining their overlapping regions.
The forward reads are specified as the argument to this option; the
reverse reads are specified with \f[V]--reverse\f[R].
Reads are matched by position: the first forward read is paired with the
first reverse read, the second with the second, and so on.
Labels are not used for matching; the run stops with a fatal error if
the two files contain different numbers of reads.
.PP
The reverse read is reverse-complemented before alignment.
Merging requires an overlap between the two reads of at least
\f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R] bases (default 10, minimum 5).
Read pairs with too many mismatches in the overlap \[em] more than
\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffs\f[R] (default 10) or more than
\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] percent (default 100.0%) \[em] are
discarded.
Additional heuristics prevent merging of read pairs that cannot be
aligned reliably.
.PP
In the merged region, quality scores from the two reads are combined
using the Phred score formula.
Outside the overlap, the quality scores from the contributing read are
used directly.
The quality score of \f[V]N\f[R] bases is replaced by the minimum score
(Q0) in every output, including the not-merged output files.
Output quality scores can be clamped with \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] (these apply only to the merged region).
Unlike the commands that pass an input quality through,
\f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] keeps the pre-2.32.0
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] default of 41, because the score it clamps is
computed rather than read: two agreeing Q40 bases have a posterior
quality of Q85, and reporting it would change the merged output of every
run.
Pass \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout 93\f[R] for the unclamped posterior.
The merged scores are written with the \f[I]input\f[R] offset,
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]: this is the only command whose output encoding
tracks its input encoding, and it does not accept
\f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R].
.PP
Staggered pairs \[em] where the 3\[cq] end of the reverse read extends
past the 5\[cq] end of the forward read \[em] are discarded by default.
Use \f[V]--fastq_allowmergestagger\f[R] to allow them; the overhanging
portions of both reads are excluded from the merged sequence, which then
covers the overlap only.
.PP
Reads can be pre-filtered with \f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_minlen\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R] (the length bounds
apply after truncation), and \f[V]--fastq_maxns\f[R]; the expected-error
filter \f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R] applies to the \f[I]merged\f[R] sequence.
Bounds on the merged sequence length are set with
\f[V]--fastq_minmergelen\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_maxmergelen\f[R].
.PP
To illustrate a merge with a 6-base overlap:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Forward (5\[aq]→3\[aq]):   AAAATTTTTT
Reverse (5\[aq]→3\[aq]):   GGGGAAAAAA

Aligned:
  Forward:      AAAATTTTTT
  Rev-comp:         TTTTTTCCCC   (reverse complement of the reverse read)

Overlap:            TTTTTT      (6 bases; scores combined)
Merged:         AAAATTTTTTCCCC
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
At the end of the run, vsearch prints a report \[em] to standard error
(unless \f[V]--quiet\f[R] is given), or to the log file when
\f[V]--log\f[R] is used \[em] giving the number of merged pairs and, for
the pairs that could not be merged, a breakdown by reason.
Most reasons correspond directly to a user-adjustable threshold:
\f[V]reads too short (after truncation)\f[R] (\f[V]--fastq_minlen\f[R]),
\f[V]reads too long (after truncation)\f[R] (\f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R]),
\f[V]too many N\[aq]s\f[R] (\f[V]--fastq_maxns\f[R]),
\f[V]too many differences\f[R] (\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffs\f[R]),
\f[V]too high percentage of differences\f[R]
(\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R]), \f[V]overlap too short\f[R]
(\f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R]), \f[V]expected error too high\f[R]
(\f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R]), \f[V]merged fragment too short\f[R]
(\f[V]--fastq_minmergelen\f[R]), \f[V]merged fragment too long\f[R]
(\f[V]--fastq_maxmergelen\f[R]), and \f[V]staggered read pairs\f[R]
(allowed with \f[V]--fastq_allowmergestagger\f[R]).
The remaining reasons reflect the alignment heuristics and are not
directly controllable:
.TP
\f[V]too few kmers found on same diagonal\f[R]
Too few k-mers shared by the two reads fall on a common alignment
diagonal, so no candidate overlap could be located and no alignment was
attempted.
.TP
\f[V]multiple potential alignments\f[R]
More than one overlap of comparable quality was found \[em] for instance
when the overlap region contains a tandem repeat \[em] so the correct
alignment is ambiguous and the pair is left unmerged.
.TP
\f[V]alignment score too low, or score drop too high\f[R]
An overlap was found and aligned, but its score remained below the
internal threshold, or the score dropped too sharply within the overlap.
This usually points to clustered mismatches or an indel in the overlap
region.
.SH ALGORITHM
.PP
Merging happens in three stages: candidate overlaps are located with
shared words, each candidate is scored by an ungapped alignment whose
scores are derived from the base qualities, and the best candidate is
accepted only if it is both good enough and unambiguous.
The thresholds below are internal and have no options of their own.
.PP
\f[B]Locating candidates.\f[R] The forward read is indexed by its 5-mers
(a shorter word than the search commands use), and every possible
overlap length is rated by the number of 5-mers the two reads share at
that offset.
Only offsets sharing at least four 5-mers are examined further.
A pair where no offset reaches that count is discarded as
\f[V]too few kmers found on same diagonal\f[R], without any alignment
being attempted.
When \f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R] is below 9 the requirement is relaxed to
\f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R] minus 4.
.PP
\f[B]Scoring a candidate.\f[R] Each surviving offset is aligned without
gaps, the forward read against the reverse complement of the reverse
read, and scored in bits as a log-odds ratio.
Writing \f[I]p\f[R] for the probability that two truly identical bases
are observed as a match, given the error probabilities \f[I]p_f\f[R] and
\f[I]p_r\f[R] of the forward and reverse base:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
p = 1 - p_f - p_r + 4 x p_f x p_r / 3
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
a matching column scores log2(\f[I]p\f[R] / 0.25) and a mismatching
column log2((1 - \f[I]p\f[R]) / 0.75), the latter capped at -4 bits so
that one disagreement between two poor bases cannot dominate.
Two good bases that agree are therefore worth almost 2 bits each, while
a disagreement between two good bases costs many.
The formulas are those of Edgar & Flyvbjerg (2015), which also give the
posterior qualities written for the merged region.
.PP
\f[B]Accepting a candidate.\f[R] The running score and its maximum so
far are tracked as the overlap is walked, and a candidate whose score
falls more than 16 bits below its own maximum is dropped: that is how a
run of clustered mismatches is rejected even when the two ends align
well.
A candidate is \f[I]acceptable\f[R] once its final score reaches 16
bits, or 1.6 times \f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R] when that is below 9 \[em]
about nine matching bases of good quality, since eight Q40 matches come
to 15.998 bits.
If more than one candidate is acceptable the overlap is ambiguous and
the pair is discarded as \f[V]multiple potential alignments\f[R], which
is what a tandem repeat in the overlap produces.
Otherwise the highest-scoring candidate is kept, and a pair whose best
candidate never reached the threshold is discarded as
\f[V]alignment score too low, or score drop too high\f[R].
.PP
Because the alignment is ungapped, an indel in the overlap is not
modelled as such: it shows up as a run of mismatches and is rejected by
the score-drop rule.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Specify the FASTQ file containing the reverse reads.
.PP
At least one output option is required: one of the merged-read outputs
below, one of the not-merged outputs
(\f[V]--fastaout_notmerged_fwd\f[R], \f[V]--fastaout_notmerged_rev\f[R],
\f[V]--fastqout_notmerged_fwd\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout_notmerged_rev\f[R]),
or \f[V]--eetabbedout\f[R] (see the following sections).
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write merged sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write merged sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq format (see
\f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when reading fastq files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.PP
The offset matters even to a command that never decodes a quality score:
it is what the reader compares the observed quality symbols against
before warning that the file may use the other encoding, it sets the
default of \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] (the highest score the offset can
represent), and the sum rules on \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] are stated in terms of it.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_minovlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the minimum length of the overlap region between the forward and
reverse reads.
Must be at least 5.
The default is 10.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffs\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximum number of mismatches allowed in the overlap region.
This option has a strong influence on the merging success rate.
The default is 10.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Set the maximum percentage of mismatches allowed in the overlap region
(0.0 to 100.0).
Additional heuristics in the merging algorithm may still discard pairs
with a high mismatch rate.
The default is 100.0.
usearch bounds the same quantity from the other side, as a minimum
identity: \f[V]--fastq_maxdiffpct\f[R] \f[I]x\f[R] is equivalent to
usearch\[cq]s \f[V]--fastq_pctid\f[R] 100 - \f[I]x\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_allowmergestagger\f[R]
Allow merging of staggered read pairs.
Staggered pairs arise when a very short fragment is sequenced: the
3\[cq] end of the reverse read extends beyond the 5\[cq] end of the
forward read.
The overhanging portion of the reverse read is discarded.
By default, staggered pairs are not merged (see
\f[V]--fastq_nostagger\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_nostagger\f[R]
Discard staggered read pairs.
This is the default behaviour.
See \f[V]--fastq_allowmergestagger\f[R] to change it.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_minmergelen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard merged sequences shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
The default is 0 (no minimum).
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxmergelen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard merged sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
The default is 1,000,000.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score higher than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not
exceed 126, the last printable ASCII character: scores up to 93 with
offset 33, up to 62 with offset 64.
The default is the highest score the offset can represent (93 with
offset 33, 62 with offset 64), so no quality score is rejected unless
this option is lowered.
Before version 2.32.0 the default was 41, the usual maximum for
Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files, which rejected PacBio HiFi and nanopore
files outright.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score lower than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be
at least 33, the first printable ASCII character: scores down to 0 with
offset 33, down to -31 with offset 64.
The value may therefore be negative, which is what the negative scores
of older formats require, but note that those formats are not supported
(see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The default is the highest score the output offset can represent (93
with \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] 33, 62 with offset 64), so scores read
from the input are written back unchanged.
Before version 2.32.0 the default was 41, the usual maximum for
Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files, which silently reduced any higher score.
Older formats may use a maximum quality score of 40.
Two commands are exceptions and keep the old default of 41, because they
\f[I]generate\f[R] the score they clamp instead of passing one through:
\f[V]--fasta2fastq\f[R], which has no input quality and uses this option
as the value to write, and \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R], which caps the
computed posterior quality of a merged base.
.PP
For \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] the offset in question is
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R], not \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]: that command
writes fastq but does not accept \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R], so a merged
quality symbol carries the same offset the input was read with.
The sum rule is stated against \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] there, and
against \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] everywhere else.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]) plus the minimum score must
be at least 33, so the value may be negative when the output offset is
64.
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
Older formats may use scores between -5 and 2.
.PP
For \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] the offset in question is
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R], not \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]: that command
writes fastq but does not accept \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R], so a merged
quality symbol carries the same offset the input was read with, and the
bound follows it.
.PP
A negative minimum matters where the score passes through vsearch and
may itself be negative: \f[V]--fastq_convert\f[R] on an offset-64 file
read with a lowered \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] writes the score back
unchanged with \f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] -5, where the default 0 would
raise it.
It has no effect where vsearch \f[I]computes\f[R] the score it clamps,
because such a score is derived from an error probability and is
therefore never negative: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R], and
\f[V]--fastq_convert --fastq_solexa\f[R], which converts to the Phred
scale before any output clamp applies.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--eeout\f[R]
Add the expected error count to each sequence header in output fasta or
fastq files, as the annotation \f[V];ee=float\f[R].
Synonym of \f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R].
Use \f[V]--xee\f[R] to remove this annotation from headers.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--eetabbedout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write expected error statistics for each successfully \f[I]merged\f[R]
pair to \f[I]filename\f[R] (pairs that could not be merged are not
reported), in a tab-separated format with four columns: the expected
errors in the forward read, the expected errors in the reverse read, the
observed differences in the forward read within the overlap region, and
the observed differences in the reverse read within the overlap region.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_notmerged_fwd\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write forward reads that could not be merged to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_notmerged_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write reverse reads that could not be merged to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R]
Add the expected error count to each sequence header in output fasta or
fastq files, as the annotation \f[V];ee=float\f[R].
Synonym of \f[V]--eeout\f[R].
Use \f[V]--xee\f[R] to remove this annotation from headers.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R] \f[I]positive real\f[R]
Discard sequences with an expected error greater than \f[I]real\f[R].
The expected error is the sum of error probabilities for all positions
in the sequence, and is strictly positive (zero or negative arguments
are rejected, as they would discard every sequence).
Applied after trimming.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Applied after trimming.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxns\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences containing more than \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
ambiguous bases (N).
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_minlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Applied after trimming.
Default is 1.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Truncate reads starting at the first base whose quality score is at or
below \f[I]positive integer\f[R].
Accepted values range from 0 to 93.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_notmerged_fwd\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write forward reads that could not be merged to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fastq format (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_notmerged_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write reverse reads that could not be merged to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fastq format (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
Messages are written \f[I]in addition\f[R] to the standard error, not
instead of it; use \f[V]--quiet\f[R] to silence the standard error copy
(see the \f[V]--quiet\f[R] entry for what it spares).
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
When nothing is left after truncation \[em] an empty \f[I]string\f[R],
or one starting with `;' or a blank character \[em] vsearch issues a
warning and writes a bare \f[V];sample=\f[R] annotation.
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
Existing \f[V];size=\f[R] annotations are reported unchanged; entries
without one receive \f[V];size=1\f[R].
For this command \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not needed: abundance annotations
are always parsed from the input headers.
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the number of computation threads to use, from 1 to 1024.
The number of threads should not exceed the number of available CPU
cores.
The value 0 is also accepted and, like the default, uses all available
cores; decimal values are truncated to their integer part.
On Linux, \[lq]available\[rq] accounts for the CPU affinity mask and the
cgroup CPU quota of the running process, so a job confined by
\f[B]taskset\f[R](1), Slurm, Docker or Kubernetes launches one thread
per core it was actually granted, rather than one per core the machine
has.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Merge paired-end reads and write merged sequences to a fastq file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_mergepairs fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse rev.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout merged.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Merge with a stricter overlap and mismatch threshold, and save unmerged
reads for inspection:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_mergepairs fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse rev.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_minovlen 20 \[rs]
    --fastq_maxdiffs 5 \[rs]
    --fastqout merged.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_notmerged_fwd unmerged_fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_notmerged_rev unmerged_rev.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Allow staggered pairs and filter on expected error after merging:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_mergepairs fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse rev.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_allowmergestagger \[rs]
    --fastq_maxee 1.0 \[rs]
    --fastqout merged.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
